# meta-deploy.list: the queue meta-deploy.bash runs, in this order.
#
# STANDING RULE (CLAUDE/AgentNotes.md): this list is exactly what needs running NOW. Add a
# run when it is pending, remove it once it has run and is done, leave out what cannot
# usefully run yet, then ask the owner to run meta-deploy.bash. Every step a run needs lives
# in the plan's own scripts, so this is the only command the owner is handed. The order is by
# dependency. A FAILED ENTRY DOES NOT STOP THE RUN: every entry runs, each one's verdict is in
# the summary, and the batch exits 1 if any failed (a plan's acceptance is skipped only when
# its own deploy failed). Only changed-plays stops inside itself, at its first failing play.
# After a run, the entries that passed are taken out and the failed ones stay for the next.
# A plan with none of triage.bash, deploy.bash and acceptance.bash has nothing
# for the script to do. A triage-only plan is run once, read-only. Words after the folder
# name are passed to that plan's triage.bash, both runs.
#
# One entry per line, four kinds:
#   <plan folder>                  its triage, deploy and acceptance scripts, in order. The
#                                  folder is named as it sits in CLAUDE/Plan/, with no
#                                  CLAUDE/Plan/ in front (00123-name, not CLAUDE/Plan/00123-name)
#   playbooks/<path>.yml [args]    a change no plan owns; run as one unit through its own
#                                  shebang, which goes through run.bash, exactly as
#                                  ./playbooks/imports/<play>.yml does by hand. Words after
#                                  it are ansible-playbook arguments: name the play's tags
#                                  (--tags lightning) so only the changed part runs. A
#                                  tagged run never decides the play's state, so a later
#                                  changed-plays still runs the play in full if it is due
#   <plan folder>/<script>.bash    one plan script, for a step none of the three fixed names
#                                  fits; run as one unit, like a playbook entry, except
#                                  that a script named *acceptance*.bash may exit 2 for
#                                  COULD NOT ESTABLISH, as an acceptance.bash may
#   changed-plays                  every play that has to run again here (one that has run
#                                  here, or one with no ledger record whose files here
#                                  show it ran), as one unit: the checkout's
#                                  ./run.bash --changed --yes, in playbook-main.yml order,
#                                  stopping at the first failure; the other plays with no
#                                  record (NEVER, UNJUDGED) are named and never run
#
# changed-plays runs WHERE IT STANDS in the list, like every other entry, and decides which
# plays it runs when it starts, so the entries above it have already run (a play an entry
# above ran is no longer changed, and is not run twice). Put it above the plan entries whose
# deploy or acceptance expects the changed plays to have run, which is the usual case, and
# below an entry it must follow. It replaces a hand-copied list of stale plays: name a
# playbooks/ entry only for a play that must run at a fixed point between two plan steps.
# Its description says which changed plays the agent expects it to pick up and why.
#
# A line starting with # is a comment and is the description of the entry below it: it is how
# the owner learns what a run will do, so write it. Blank lines are ignored. Any other line
# that is not an entry is a fatal error naming its line number.

# Issues #393, #391, #396, #395 and #394 (ccy 3.124.30, container 2.84.71). #393: ccy-teams takes a teamchat seat only for a
# session with a ccy session record. A ccy --teams (or cc --teams) launch from a pane already
# on ccy's tmux, or with no terminal, has no record, so the seat sweep released its seat about a
# minute after it started; such a launch now stops at once with exit 78, naming the starts that
# write a record (ccy or cc at a terminal outside ccy's tmux, or ccy-sessions declare then
# ccy-sessions restore). A session's retry of its own take that stopped part way (homeserver
# down) now goes on instead of finding the seat held. The image is rebuilt once for its
# teamchat. The check applies to launches after this run; a running session is unchanged.
# #391: the host tools that wait for a Claude Code session to be idle (ccy-sessions set-going
# and wind-down, ccy --stop, the team restarts) now see a turn running in the newer Claude Code
# screen layout, whose interrupt hint is in the mode line below the input box, so they no
# longer type into a running turn (ccy-sessions, ccy-teams, the claude-yolo front and
# ccy-token-usable are rebuilt). #396 (ccy 3.124.25): ccy-sessions restore takes session names
# and judges only those, so a stale record no longer fails the restore of another session; a
# stale record's message says how to remove it, with the new verb ccy-sessions forget NAME; and
# restore --dry-run prints "would fail <name>: <why>" for each record that would fail. #395:
# ccy-sessions verify-restore says, for a session still SETTING-GOING that set-going has not
# typed into, whether a set-going is running to do it or that none is.
# #394 (ccy 3.124.30, container 2.84.71): a teamchat hook input that could not be used is named
# by its hook event, reason and byte count (never its content) in link.log, session.json,
# status.json and the status line, so the next one seen after a restore says which hook it was.
# The image is rebuilt once more for its teamchat; sessions started after the rebuild show it.
playbooks/imports/play-claude-yolo.yml

# Plan 00257 B.12 proof. Its checks are read-only: in each declared LXC member container that
# is running, the session units are present, not enabled and not linked in
# multi-user.target.wants. A stopped container is reported offline and skipped. It then ASKS,
# per running container, whether to reboot it (default no; asked at the terminal even under
# meta-deploy) and reboots only on the owner's yes, then checks no claude runs for its members'
# users. Exit 2 means it could not establish the answer: nothing judged (every container
# offline, say), fewer units found than members declared, a probe that did not answer, or a
# failed lxc-stop or lxc-start.
00257-cross-host-lxc-team-members/lxc-no-autostart-acceptance.bash
# Issue #322 (ccy 3.124.18): line-based listings are captured first instead of read through a
# process substitution and its wait "$!". Besides the launcher, cc and agent-bus-install (whose
# plays have run), the change reaches the user tools imgpaste, qnap-finder, raw-prune,
# ssh-agent-start, ssh-keys-rekey and ssh-with-agent.bash, fedora-desktop-self-update,
# docker-in-lxc and the host-health kernel-change script. changed-plays is expected to pick up
# the plays that deploy those.
changed-plays
